        "knowledge_point": "Determining the Range of a Parametric Equation",
        "problem": "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t^2-6t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^4+4, \\quad t \\in (-\\infty , \\infty),\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$",
        "worked_solution": "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2}-6t \\\\  & ={(t-3)}^{2}-(3)^{2} \\\\  & ={(t-3)}^{2}-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\left( t-3\\right)^2 \\geq 0,$ we can conclude that $x \\geq -9.$\n\n- To find the range of $y=t^4+4,$ notice that $t^4 \\geq 0,$ and consequently $y \\geq 4.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $x \\geq -9$ and $y \\geq 4.$",
